nt', e.g., '2: 4' means there are four 2s left unknown)\n2: 1, A: 1, Q: 1, J: 1, 10: 2, 9: 1, 8: 2, 7: 1, 5: 1, 4: 1\nValid Moves:\npair: ♥K ♣K\nsingle: ♠7\nsingle: ♣K\n\nSelect the best move from the Valid Moves list above. You can choose to pass even if you have valid moves that can beat the previous play, but you cannot pass if you are in a Free Play (no last play).\nEnsure your response matches one of the valid moves exactly.\nRemember to provide Explanation, Reason, and Answer.", 'reasoning': '', 'output': 'Evaluation: I have three options: play a single card (♠7 or ♣K) or play a pair (♥K ♣K). Since the last play was by me (♣A), and both opponents passed, this is now a free play — meaning I must play something. I cannot pass. My goal as a peasant is to help my teammate win by either getting rid of my cards efficiently or setting up plays that allow my teammate (who still has 8 cards) to take control.\n\nPlaying the pair ♥K ♣K would be a strong move because it uses two high cards that are hard for the landlord (who only has 4 cards left) to beat. Kings are high-ranking, so unless the landlord holds Aces or 2s (which are limited — only one 2 and one Ace unaccounted for), they may not be able to beat it. This could hand control to my teammate, who can then continue leading with smaller cards to burn through their hand.\n\nAlternatively, playing a single card like ♠7 or ♣K keeps more flexibility, but since I only have three cards left, flexibility is less important than forcing a favorable sequence. Playing a single King might force the landlord to respond with a higher card (like an Ace or 2), which could help my teammate later. But if I play the pair, I’m using a combination that only a higher pair (like Aces or 2s) can beat — and given the remaining unknowns (only one Ace and one 2 left unseen), it’s unlikely the landlord has a high pair.\n\nReason: Playing the pair ♥K ♣K maximizes pressure on the landlord while minimizing the chance they can follow suit. It also clears two cards from my hand, bringing me closer to winning. If the landlord cannot beat it, control goes to my teammate, who can then lead with lower cards to drain their own hand. This supports our shared objective.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": ["♥K", "♣K"]}'}
